(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-introduction-bias-validity-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Introduction, Bias and Validity Quiz 2 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How should a clinician judge whether they have good information?","choices":["By checking whether it agrees with what they already believe","By counting how many colleagues share the same view","By critically analysing it and distinguishing strong from weak","By accepting anything published in a medical journal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and two other things bear on it: a trusting patient-provider relationship, and the quality of the source material behind the provider's own knowledge. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"In the five As framework, what does Ask mean?","choices":["Systematically retrieve the available literature","Assess validity and applicability","Define the clinical question","Evaluate outcomes on patient care"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the example given is whether one treatment is better than another. Everything after it depends on the question being defined first. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What characterises a state list of reportable diseases?","choices":["It is limited to sexually transmitted infections","It is limited to diseases that can be prevented by vaccination","It is identical in every state","It is long and varied, spanning infections, poisonings and cancers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A single state list runs from acquired immunodeficiency syndrome and anthrax through arsenic and carbon monoxide poisoning to cancer and congenital anomalies. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which database is named as the source for literature reviews?","choices":["MEDLINE","The Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews","PubMed","The National Health Interview Survey"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Original articles are found instead through MEDLINE and PubMed, so the choice of database follows from whether you want a synthesis or the primary studies. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What are the final two steps of the five As?","choices":["Apply, then Assess","Assess, then Apply","Appraise, then Acquire","Ask, then Acquire"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: changes are made to practice, and then outcomes on patient care are evaluated. Skipping the last step leaves you without evidence that the change helped. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is evidence-based medicine?","choices":["Following national guidelines without individual judgement","Applying clinical epidemiology to patient care","Basing decisions on the recommendation of a senior colleague","Collecting original data on every patient before treating them"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it has three moves: using clinical judgement to assess available research for validity and applicability, then applying that evidence to decisions about care. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What distinguishes primary from secondary research?","choices":["Primary research is published first, secondary research later","Primary research uses data the researcher collects personally","Primary research is always a randomised trial","Secondary research involves a second group of participants"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and secondary research analyses data that already exists. Original research articles are the example of the first; meta-analyses and systematic reviews of the second. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which of these is named as a contribution of epidemiology to medicine?","choices":["Synthesising new pharmaceutical compounds","Performing surgical procedures more safely","Determining the natural history of disease","Writing the medical record for an individual encounter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The list also includes investigating new disease transmission, finding preventable causes, studying the biological spectrum of disease, evaluating community interventions, setting control priorities and providing expert testimony. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which three inputs combine to produce good clinical decision making?","choices":["Clinical expertise, external evidence, and patient values","Clinical expertise, institutional policy and cost containment","External evidence, statistical significance and journal reputation","Patient values, provider confidence and peer consensus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the output named is not merely a decision but improved patient care, which is the point of combining all three rather than relying on any one. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is described as eminence-based medicine?","choices":["Substituting volume and stridency for evidence","Senior colleagues holding experience to trump evidence","Sartorial elegance and verbal eloquence","Fear of litigation driving over-investigation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it heads a list of alternatives to evidence that each substitute something else for appraisal: volume, eloquence, timidity, fear of litigation or bravado. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is the Behavioral Risk Factor Surveillance Survey?","choices":["A registry of cancer cases maintained by state government","A mandatory report filed by clinicians for notifiable diseases","A telephone survey of health-related risk behaviours","A physical examination survey conducted in mobile clinics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it also captures chronic health conditions among United States residents, which makes it a behavioural and chronic-disease instrument rather than a registration system. It covers preventive service use as well. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is the attrition effect, and what is its consequence?","choices":["Participants drop out of the study, which reduces the sample size","Participants are allocated to groups that differ at baseline","Survey respondents differ systematically from those who do not respond","Investigators withhold a subset of their data"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and a smaller sample is the immediate consequence, which matters because a study's ability to detect a real difference depends on how many participants remain. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is the independent variable in a study?","choices":["The possible effect","A variable that may distort the relationship between the other two","The subset of people actually studied","The possible cause"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the dependent variable is the possible effect. Naming which is which is the first step in reading what a study actually claims. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"When does selection bias occur?","choices":["When the method of measurement leads to incorrect results","When participants drop out of the study before it ends","When research without significant results is less likely to be published","When compared groups differ in more ways than the study's focus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the consequence is severe: few conclusions can be drawn about the topic of interest, because the difference in outcome cannot be attributed to the thing being studied. What matters is that those extra differences affect the outcome. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"At which stages of a study can bias occur?","choices":["Data collection only","Conception and design, collection, analysis and interpretation","Only after the results have been published","Only when the investigators have a financial conflict of interest"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is why appraising a study means reading the whole of it: a sound design can still be undone by how the data were gathered, analysed or read. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What is the particular value of a disease registry?","choices":["It measures self-reported behaviour in a representative sample","It provides information about trends over time","It registers every birth and death in the jurisdiction","It establishes the cause of a disease directly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Registries are usually established by state government to collect disease-specific data, and because collection continues, they show how a disease behaves across years rather than at one moment. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is a conflict of interest, and what should be done about it?","choices":["A disagreement between investigators about how to analyse the data","An imbalance between the intervention and control groups","Financial or personal considerations affect objectivity","A study whose results contradict those of an earlier study"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the reason disclosure rather than exclusion is the remedy is that the impact may be unconscious as well as conscious, so the reader is left to weigh it. Authors should disclose it. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"On what does good medical decision making depend?","choices":["The provider's clinical experience alone","Published evidence alone, independent of the patient","The cost of the intervention above all other factors","Good information, from history, examination and knowledge"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it also relies on patient goals and values, provider beliefs, and the risks, benefits, effectiveness, adverse reactions and cost of any intervention. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What can give rise to confounding?","choices":["Publication bias alone","Attrition alone","Failure to blind the outcome assessor","Selection bias or chance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and this is why it is an important consideration during data analysis: whether it arose by biased sampling or by bad luck, the analysis is where it has to be detected and addressed. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What does descriptive research produce?","choices":["Careful observation and a description of what is found","An account of why a relationship exists","A test of whether a larger study would be feasible","A pooled estimate drawn from several published studies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the examples given are concrete: disease rates for a given population, and the demographic characteristics of those who have the disease. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What is measurement bias?","choices":["The method of measurement leads to incorrect results","The groups compared differ in more ways than the study's focus","The effect of a covariate cannot be separated from the effect under study","Only a subset of the relevant data is made available"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the example given is blood pressure: if standardised procedures are not followed for every measurement taken during a study, the resulting values cannot be trusted. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Why might a sponsoring company contribute to publication bias?","choices":["It insists that all its funded studies be published in full","It is less likely to support research unfavourable to its product","It requires investigators to disclose conflicts of interest","It selects participants who are most likely to benefit"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it sits alongside journal editor preference as the second force keeping results that show nothing out of the literature. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which are given as examples of secondary research?","choices":["Original research articles","Questionnaires and surveys","Meta-analyses and systematic review articles","Laboratory test results and vital signs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because both analyse data that already exists rather than collecting new data. Original research articles are the example of primary research. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which clinical question does the term Diagnosis address?","choices":["What conditions lead to the disease?","How does treatment change the course of the disease?","How accurate are the tests used to identify the disease?","Does early detection improve the course of the disease?"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The question is about the performance of the tests themselves, which is why test properties are studied as a topic in their own right rather than assumed. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A trial compares surgical with medical therapy. Patients in the surgical arm were healthy enough to tolerate an operation, while the medical arm carried more comorbidities. Which bias does this illustrate?","choices":["Selection bias","Measurement bias","Publication bias","Non-response bias"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The groups differ in baseline health as well as in treatment, so a difference in outcome cannot be attributed to the therapy, which is the defining problem of biased sampling. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which international body publishes the Demographic Yearbook?","choices":["The World Health Organization","The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention","The National Center for Health Statistics","The United Nations"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it carries demographic and social statistics. The World Health Organization publishes the separate World Health Statistics Manual, covering health statistics for its member states. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"At which stage of a study is selection bias an important consideration?","choices":["Data analysis","Data interpretation","Publication","Study design"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because how the groups are assembled is decided then. Confounding by contrast is an important consideration during data analysis, once the data exist. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What does clinical epidemiology do?","choices":["Describes disease frequency in a population without reference to individuals","Makes predictions about individual patients from events in groups of similar patients","Replaces the history and physical examination with statistical reasoning","Studies disease mechanisms in laboratory animal models"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the qualifier matters: strong scientific methods are what make those predictions accurate, so the method by which the group was studied determines how much the prediction is worth. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A series of blood pressure measurements in a study all fall above the true value. What does that pattern indicate?","choices":["Bias, because the error is systematic not random","Chance, because measurement always varies","Confounding by an unmeasured variable","Attrition, because participants left the study"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Random variation would scatter the readings above and below the true value with equal likelihood, so a consistent one-sided departure points to systematic error. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the purpose of exploratory research?","choices":["To address a new or understudied topic and satisfy curiosity","To observe carefully and describe what is found","To attempt to explain why something occurs","To analyse data that another researcher has already collected"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it has two further purposes that are practical rather than intellectual: testing the feasibility of a more careful study and developing the methods that study would use. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"}]};
